me</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Pat Freiermuth</strong> has room to work \u2014 CIN allow <strong>46 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Tee Higgins</strong> (WR2) \u2014 PIT leak 42 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Mike Gesicki</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 PIT are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.39 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Pat Freiermuth</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 CIN are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.47 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CIN throw it (64% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>CIN \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CIN stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>49%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Chase Brown</strong> (13 carries, 57 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 81 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><h3>PIT \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>PIT are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jaylen Warren</strong> (13 carries, 61 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><